id: pm-rl-keet
title: "pm rl invalidate: transitive result invalidation over the pm graph"
description: "Given an environment or benchmark version, list every EvalResult, Transfer and Run transitively invalidated by changing it. This is the question that costs programmes weeks and that nothing else records, and it is a reachability query over dependencies pm already stores."
type: Feature
status: in_progress
priority: 2
tags: []
created_at: "2026-07-31T06:38:36.725Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-16T02:27:55.738Z"
assignee: pi-agent
claim_principal: pi-agent
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 480
acceptance_criteria: Returns exactly the transitive set with no false positives and no omissions on a fixture with several generations of environment versions; reports the path by which each result is reached
dependencies[1]{id,kind,created_at,author,source_kind,author_source}:
  pm-rl-e20d,parent,"2026-07-31T06:38:37.062Z","harness:claude-code","cli:update:dep",detected
comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-16T01:48:01.075Z",pi-agent,"Starting implementation. Plan: `pm rl invalidate <id>` walks the host-owned relationship graph (`client.graph(\"impact\", {id}, {direction: \"incoming\"})`) so invalidation is genuine reachability over the `dependencies` edges pm already stores — Run→Environment, EvalResult→Run/Benchmark, Transfer→both environments — not a re-implementation in pm-rl. It classifies affected items into EvalResult/Transfer/Run and reports the exact path for each. Adversarial tests: cycle, dangling dep id, zero-overlap runs, identical-metrics-different-config, and cross-environment-version refusal for compare."
  "2026-08-16T02:27:55.738Z",pi-agent,"Critical finding while implementing: the host's `pm graph impact` registers the `related` kind (what `--dep` records) as an UNDIRECTED relationship, so the blast-radius walk crosses a Transfer's second environment into the other version's runs — reporting results whose provenance does not derive from the changed version. Invalidation is directional data flow (a result derives FROM its dependencies), so the walk runs over the `dependencies` arrays pm itself stores (one `list --fields id,type,dependencies` read) with a directed BFS toward dependents. pm-rl still stores no graph of its own: the edge data, kinds, and dangling references all come from pm's items. Exactness proven on a real fixture: invalidating env v1 returns exactly v1's subtree plus the shared transfer, never v2/v3's runs."
body: ""
